implementationPlan.description:
VERIFY ON THE LANE THAT ACTUALLY COVERS THIS, AND THE ADVERSARIAL PASS.

THE VERIFICATION FOR THIS ROW IS THE SECURITY LANE, and quoting a daemon count line proves nothing about it — that is precisely how this defect shipped green. Run `npx vitest run --config tests/security/vitest.config.ts tests/security/boundary-roster.security.ts` and quote the count line verbatim: it must go from `Tests 3 failed | 52 passed (55)` to all 55 passing.
Then run the full security lane (`pnpm test:security`) and quote its count line, since the four coupled constants are read by arms beyond the one file.
Also run `pnpm --filter @moe/daemon test` and `pnpm typecheck`: the module and its test changed, and those two lanes are what cover the daemon side even though neither would have caught the original red.

ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA, since the DoD field is the placeholder "Task completed as described" and grades nothing. These are what this row must show, and they are recorded in a row comment for QA:
(a) the three constants no longer match `^export const` and no production module imports them;
(b) the exported scan is exactly 180 with `EXPECTED_ROSTER_SIZE` UNEDITED;
(c) all four private-population constants moved together and the header prose that states the arithmetic moved with them;
(d) the rewritten test arms still FAIL when a code is remapped to the wrong layer — demonstrate it, do not assert it;
(e) the security lane is green and its count line is quoted.

ADVERSARIAL PASS (load `adversarial-self-review`). Ask, in order: after the rewrite, can the roster-coverage arm still notice a code remapped to the wrong layer — mutate one entry and watch it red, because an arm that cannot fail has been deleted rather than rewritten? Is there any OTHER module under apps that declares a layer constant the exported roster does not know about, or did this row fix one instance of a class? Does `pnpm test:security` run in any gate that a normal row's DoD would name — and if not, is that worth a comment on this row, since it is the reason this shipped? And the question behind the row: is `PREVIEW_AUTO_DECISION` genuinely a layer that never reaches a wire, or does it reach one through a path nobody has traced — preview-auto-composition.ts is the place to look.
